!!id:"https://tson.io/test-suite/class2/link/valid/an-api-description-declares-its-endpoint-shapes-beside-its-operations-expected.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/test-suite/schemas/link-sidecar.tn"
{
  spec: "[TSON-SCHEMA] §3.4.1 (the wait order, proposed), [TSON-SCHEMA] §4.1, [TSON-SCHEMA] §4.2, [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.7, [TSON-SCHEMA] §5.12"
  description: """
    An API description under one level, the shape the http project relies on: one schema imports an HTTP vocabulary
    and an ordinary schema of records, and declares its own types, its own endpoint shape and its operations side by
    side, with no meta layer. `paged_get` refines the imported record type `operation` and pins `method`, marked so
    a value may omit it; `list_orders` is a value entry read against `paged_get`, waiting on it as `paged_get`
    waits on the import, and writes no `method`, while `place_order` is a value of `operation` itself. A
    response's `body` is a `type_kind` slot: `order_page`, an alias closed from a template the same schema
    declares, and `order`, from the import, are both judged members of it. `orders` is a value of the namespace
    type `api`, and the binding's `ref<method>` resolves into it by a qualified name. `list_orders` fills a
    `ref<paged_get>` and, through `paged_get`'s IS-A edge, a `ref<operation>` beside `place_order`.
    """
  valid: {
    binds: [page order_page paged_get list_orders place_order orders post_order catalog]
  }
}
